    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🕛 The daily cut-off</h3>
        <p>Stake and unstake requests don't happen instantly. They queue up and all run once a day (around midnight ET)
          at that day's price. That way nobody can jump in just before a lotto draw and jump out right after.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>💸 Getting your PAT out</h3>
        <p>A withdrawal needs free cash in that vault: Bank money may be lent out, and the House always keeps a minimum float
          to pay winners. If there isn't enough, your request waits in the queue and fills as cash frees up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>📏 Limits</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Stakers can own at most <b>50%</b> of any vault, so the protocol always keeps skin in the game.</li>
          <li><b>Protocol dividend:</b> once a vault is bigger than its target size (the House: 20M; the Bank and Market maker: 1.5× what they have lent or committed), the protocol cashes in its <i>own</i> earnings and pays them to the Federal Reserve, where they fund rewards and insurance. Stakers' shares are never touched.</li>
          <li>Optional <b>ceiling</b>: stakers' return above 25% a year <span class="cfg">⚙</span> goes back to the Federal Reserve.</li>
          <li>Optional <b>floor</b>: the Reserve can top stakers up <span class="cfg">⚙</span> (off by default).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="h3s">⚖️ Fees follow risk</h3>
    <p class="lead">A fee goes to whoever put up the money for that activity. Market fees go to the Market maker that seeded
      the market, loan interest goes to the Bank that lent it, casino margins go to the House. Fees with no money behind them
      (wager and bounty fees, fines, purchases, paid commands) go to the Federal Reserve or the jackpot, by the
      routing table. Stakers don't get paid for activity they don't fund.</p>

    <h3 class="h3s">🎟️ The Lotto pot</h3>
    <p class="lead">Each 1,000 PAT lotto ticket splits in two: <b>40%</b> builds the Lotto pot, a separate rolling jackpot that only
      the lotto jackpot is paid from (so a jackpot win never drains the House), and <b>60%</b> goes to the House, which pays
      the smaller fixed prizes and keeps the margin. The jackpot is capped at 100M.</p>

